#{[17592186119619 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ if there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -21\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -21\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+8{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0x_1 + 0x_2 = 3,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{b}$ does *not* lie in $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}.$"] [17592186119620 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\Pi$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s+2t &=2 \\\\ 4s+4t &=10 \\\\ 5s+6t &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s+2t &=2 \\\\ -4t &=2 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t = 2 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad t = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s+2t & =2 \\\\ s+2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =2 \\\\ s-1 & =2 \\\\ s & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=3, \\, t=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $\\Pi.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=3\\mathbf{b} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{c}.\n$$"] [17592186119671 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ if there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=13 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -70 \\\\ 7 & 2 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -70 \\\\ 0 & 16 & k-91\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{14}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 16 & k-91\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-16{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-11\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0x_1 + 0x_2 = k-11$. Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0 = k-11 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k=11.\n$$"] [17592186120294 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186120294 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-7 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & 1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k-28\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k-28\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-3.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-3\\,⇒\\,k=3.\n$$"] [17592186120303 :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$, and $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186120303 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=-6 \\\\ -7{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=-11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -7 & -5 & -11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ -7 & -5 & -11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -18\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -18\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-9{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+(-2){\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot (-2)=-6.$"] [17592186120304 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},$ $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,5,8⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-1,-1,-3⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨-3,5,-7⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186120306 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}$, $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,3,5⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,-5,-7⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨1,-8,-10⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186120306 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t &=1 \\\\ 3s-5t &=-8 \\\\ 5s-7t &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -8 \\\\ 5 & -7 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -7 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t &=1 \\\\ -2t &=-5 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2t=-5\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+t & =1 \\\\ -s+\\frac{5}{2} & =1 \\\\ -s & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ s & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=\\frac{3}{2},\\,t=\\frac{5}{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}+\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle s\\cdot t=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186120355 :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$, and $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186120355 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=12,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{b}$ does *not* lie in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}.$"] [17592186120516 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -10 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186120516 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -10 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=-12 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2} &=-10 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 9 & -12 \\\\ -2 & 7 & -10 \\\\ -6 & 18 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 7 & -10 \\\\ -6 & 18 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 18 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+24\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k+24.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k+24\\,⇒\\,k=-24.\n$$"] [17592186121035 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n$$\n\\mathbf{a_1} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\t\\mathbf{a_2} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} .\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b} & ={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- {x}_{1} + 3 {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} + 2 {x}_{2} \\\\ - {x}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]} \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)"] [17592186121037 :tutorial/content "The **column space** of a matrix $A$ is denoted $\\text{Col}(A)$ and is the span of the columns of $A$ (i.e., the set of all possible linear combinations of columns of $A$). For example, consider the matrix\n\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}1} & {\\color{blue}3} \\\\ {\\color{red}2} & {\\color{blue}- 1}\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nwhere we denote its columns by $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ respectively. The column space of this matrix is given by\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A) = \n\t\\textrm{Span}\n\t\t\\Bigg\\{\n\t\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}1} \\\\ {\\color{red}2}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1},\n\t\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{blue}3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}- 1}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\n\t\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we would like to determine whether the vector $\\mathbf{b} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ In other words, we need to check if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.$ So, we need to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as some linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation above can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}} & {\\color{blue}{{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{1}} & {\\color{blue}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{\\text{x}}} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{\\text{b}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation is equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$ To solve the equation, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -7\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -7{x}_{2} &=-7.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get $x_1=-1.$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\n\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{b}} = \n\t(-1) \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}1} \\\\ {\\color{red}2}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a_1}} + \n\t1 \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{blue}3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}- 1}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a_2}}\n$$"] [17592186121042 :tutorial/content "Given a subspace $H$ with a basis $\\mathcal B = \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\}$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in H,$ the **coordinates of the vector** $\\mathbf{x}$ **relative to the basis** $\\mathcal{B}$ are the coefficients ${\\color{red} x_1}$ and ${\\color{blue} x_2}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}x_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = \\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the vector $\\mathbf x$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal B$ using these coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}{x}_{1}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{x}_{2}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ where \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nAlso let $H = \\textrm{span} \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\},$ and consider the vector $\\mathbf{x} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\\in H.$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf x$ can be expressed as a unique linear combination of $\\mathbf b_1$ and $\\mathbf b_2.$ That is to say, there exist *unique coefficients* $\\color{red}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}{{x}_{1}}}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{\\color{blue}{{x}_{2}}}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {\\color{red}{{x}_{1}}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{\\color{blue}{{x}_{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve for these coefficients. From the above, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{\\color{red}{{x}_{1}}}+{\\color{blue}{{x}_{2}}} &=8 \\\\ 2{\\color{blue}{{x}_{2}}} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of this system is \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}{x}_{1}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{x}_{2}}\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = {\\color{red}3} \\mathbf{b}_1+ {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/94/94b2934727a32284e9790e59ccd301bd858a0258843314a2968285a5eda7a302.png)"] [17592186121126 :tutorial/content "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed  under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words: \n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$ \n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n$$\nL = \\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: y=3x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form ${\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ 3 x\\end{array} \\right]},$ where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3 {u}_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3 {v}_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}.$ If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{{u}_{1}}}+{\\color{red}{{v}_{1}}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot ({\\color{red}{{u}_{1}}}+{\\color{red}{{v}_{1}}})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3 {u}_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot \\mathbf{u} & =c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{c{u}_{1}}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{c{u}_{1}}}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: y=x+1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$"] [17592186121127 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ \n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n$$\n\\mathbf{d}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]} -   {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}  = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]} .\n$$\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 {u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ - 2 {u}_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 {v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ - 2 {v}_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}.$ If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot ({\\color{red}{{u}_{1}}}+{\\color{red}{{v}_{1}}}) \\\\ {\\color{red}{{u}_{1}}}+{\\color{red}{{v}_{1}}} \\\\ ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot ({\\color{red}{{u}_{1}}}+{\\color{red}{{v}_{1}}})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 {u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ - 2 {u}_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot \\mathbf{u} & =c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{c{u}_{1}}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{c{u}_{1}}} \\\\ ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot {\\color{red}{c{u}_{1}}}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$"] [17592186121128 :tutorial/content "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ 4 x - 2 y\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad x,y \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4 {u}_{1} - 2 {u}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4 {v}_{1} - 2 {v}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}.$ If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{{u}_{1}}}+{\\color{red}{{v}_{1}}} \\\\ {\\color{violet}{{u}_{2}}}+{\\color{violet}{{v}_{2}}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot ({\\color{red}{{u}_{1}}}+{\\color{red}{{v}_{1}}})-{\\color{blue}{2}}\\cdot ({\\color{violet}{{u}_{2}}}+{\\color{violet}{{v}_{2}}})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4 {u}_{1} - 2 {u}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot \\mathbf{u} & =c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}{c{u}_{1}}} \\\\ {\\color{violet}{c{u}_{2}}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{c{u}_{1}}}-{\\color{blue}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{violet}{c{u}_{2}}}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$"] [17592186135177 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a square $n\\times n$ matrix $Q$ with columns $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{q}_n{:}$\n\n$$\nQ = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}| & | &  & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} & \\cdots  & {\\mathbf{q}}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $Q$ is said to be **orthogonal** if its columns $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\dots, \\mathbf{q}_n$ form an *orthonormal* set. In other words, \n\n- the columns of $Q$ must be *mutually orthogonal*, and\n\n- they must all be *unit* vectors.\n\nFor instance, the matrix \n$$\nQ={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{black}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}} & {\\color{black}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}}} \\\\ {\\color{black}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}}} & {\\color{black}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \nis orthogonal since its columns \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}_1}={\\color{black}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}}\\end{array} \\right]}}, \n\\qquad\n{\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}_2}={\\color{black}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\\end{array} \\right]}}\n$$\n\nform an orthonormal set $\\left\\{ {\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}_1}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}_2} \\right\\}.$ To see this, note the following:\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf q_1$ and $\\mathbf q_2$ are orthogonal, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{black}{{\\mathbf{q}}_{1}}}\\cdot {\\color{black}{{\\mathbf{q}}_{2}}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Both $\\mathbf q_1$ and $\\mathbf q_2$ are unit vectors, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{\\mathbf{q}}_{1}|{|}^{2} & ={\\mathbf{q}}_{1}\\cdot {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{\\mathbf{q}}_{2}|{|}^{2} & ={\\mathbf{q}}_{2}\\cdot {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186163254 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if \n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\nthen the product $A\\cdot B$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{\\times }}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons."] [17592186241011 :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$, and $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186241011 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=-17\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -5 & -17\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3(-2)=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=9.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+(-2){\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 9\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=9\\cdot (-2)=-18.$"] [17592186241017 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186241017 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=8 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2} &=-7 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 4 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 4 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+8\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k+8.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k+8\\,⇒\\,k=-8.\n$$"] [17592186241019 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186241019 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} &=-6 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 12 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+9\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k+9.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k+9\\,⇒\\,k=-9.\n$$"] [17592186241021 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},$ $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,3,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,-1,-1⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨5,-5,-4⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186241021 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+2t &=5 \\\\ 3s-t &=-5 \\\\ 2s-t &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+2t &=5 \\\\ t &=2 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+2t & =5 \\\\ -s+2(2) & =5 \\\\ -s+4 & =5 \\\\ s & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=-1,\\,t=2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=-\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=(-1)\\cdot 2=-2.$"]}
